#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125849 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a stochastic matrix $P$ and a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}.$ Then, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a **steady-state vector** of $P$ if \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nFor example, to show that the stochastic vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nis a steady-state vector for the stochastic matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nwe compute the matrix product $P\\mathbf q\\mathbin:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.94+2\\cdot 0.03 \\\\ 0.06+2\\cdot 0.97\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =q\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P\\mathbf q = \\mathbf q,$ the vector $\\mathbf q$ is a steady-state vector of $P.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e1601d67-32bc-5796-a800-6e704f25f991" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5500 :tutorial/title "Steady-State Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125850 :tutorial/content "To find a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P,$ we seek a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nLet's begin by writing this equation in a slightly different form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector we seek is a solution to the matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's use this idea to find a steady-state vector for the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $(P-I):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, to solve $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0},$ we reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1= \\dfrac 23 x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "78f635ff-e2e7-5c9f-9ec2-470187a45bc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5501 :tutorial/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125851 :tutorial/content "A stochastic matrix $P$ is **regular** if there exists a positive integer $n$ such that $P^n$ has all positive elements.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- The stochastic matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nhas all positive elements. Therefore, it is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\n- The stochastic matrix $Q,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \ncontains a zero element. However, if we compute its square, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nwhich has all positive elements. Therefore, $Q$ is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\nRegular stochastic matrices have the following important property:\n\n> *Any regular stochastic matrix has a **unique** steady-state vector $\\mathbf q.$ Moreover, the Markov chain\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> converges to $\\mathbf q$ for **any** initial state $\\mathbf x_0.$*\n\nIn other words, if [math]P[/math] is a regular [math]m \\times m[/math] stochastic matrix and [math]\\mathbf{q}_0 \\in \\mathbf{R}^m[/math] is an arbitrary (initial) stochastic vector, then [math]P[/math] has the unique steady state vector [math]\\mathbf{q}[/math] and \n\n\\[\nP^n \\mathbf{q}_0 \\to \\mathbf{q} \n\\]\n\nas [math]n \\to \\infty.[/math]" :tutorial/id #uuid "255fadf0-9fc2-52d5-976b-015a97f52b08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5502 :tutorial/title "Regular Stochastic Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125855 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nAdditionally, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside at the time of the study. \n\nWe can represent this situation using a stochastic matrix $P$ and initial state vector $\\mathbf x_0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs we've seen, to find the state vector $\\mathbf x_{k+1}$ that corresponds to the $(k+1)$th year, we left-multiply the state vector corresponding to the $k$th year by $P.$ This gives rise to the following Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe local authority wants to determine whether the state vectors settle down to a fixed state. With the aid of a computer, they compute the following states:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.636\\,97 \\\\ 0.363\\,03\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.609\\,64 \\\\ 0.390\\,36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{100} & =P{x}_{99}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.333\\,363 \\\\ 0.666\\,637\\end{bmatrix}\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{101} & =P{x}_{100}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.333\\,36 \\\\ 0.666\\,64\\end{bmatrix}\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the data, we see that the vectors $\\mathbf x_{100}$ and $\\mathbf x_{101}$ are both approximately equal to the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFurthermore, computing $\\mathbf x_{102}, \\mathbf{x}_{103}, \\ldots$ all give vectors approximately equal to $\\mathbf q.$ This suggests that the sequence $\\mathbf x_k$ converges to $\\mathbf q\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_k \\rightarrow \\mathbf q \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad k\\to\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf q$ is called a **steady-state vector** of the matrix $P.$ Here, our steady-state vector suggests that after enough time has passed, one-third of the population will live in the city and two-thirds will live in the countryside.\n\nFinally, notice that for large values of $k,$ we have\n\n$$\nP\\mathbf x_k \\approx \\mathbf x_{k}.\n$$\n\nThis observation indicates how to find $\\mathbf q$ without resorting to computers." :tutorial/id #uuid "cb9435c4-44ed-58ec-9c04-b8fed4403fcd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6151 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
